2. Critical Principle

Firebase is the identity provider.

MongoDB is the application database.

Do not mix these responsibilities.

Firebase:

Authentication.

Identity.

UID.

Email verification.

Password reset.

Authentication providers.

Session/token identity.

MongoDB:

Customer profile.

Application roles.

Destinations.

Packages.

Enquiries.

Bookings.

Favourites.

Reviews.

Blogs.

Gallery.

Services.

Site settings.

Audit data.

3. Authentication Architecture

Client

Use Firebase Web SDK.

signIn
   ↓
Firebase Auth
   ↓
Firebase User
   ↓
ID Token

For server session:

ID Token
   ↓
POST /api/auth/session
   ↓
Firebase Admin verifyIdToken()
   ↓
createSessionCookie()
   ↓
Set-Cookie

Firebase officially supports this server-side session-cookie pattern.citeturn0search3

Cookie requirements:

HttpOnly.

Secure in production.

SameSite appropriate for deployment.

Limited lifetime.

Clear on logout.

Revoke when necessary.

4. Authentication Verification

For protected requests:

Request
  ↓
Read session cookie
  ↓
Firebase Admin verifySessionCookie()
  ↓
Firebase UID
  ↓
MongoDB User.findOne({ firebaseUid })
  ↓
Check status
  ↓
Check role
  ↓
Authorize

Firebase also supports direct server-side ID-token verification throughthe Admin SDK. citeturn0search1

Never trust:

req.body.userId
req.body.role
clientRole
localStorageRole

